            <ArticleTitle>Obesity and cancer--the update 2013.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>219-27</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.beem.2013.04.005</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1521-690X(13)00037-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Progress of the last five years regarding &quot;Obesity and Cancer&quot; with preference to cohort studies was reviewed for cancer of the colorectum, breast, endometrium, renal cell, and adenocarcinomas of the esophagus and compared to the knowledge reviewed in the year 2008. The new studies are mostly confirming what has been known also 5 years ago. Gender seems to play a role in colorectal cancer in that risk due to body fatness is much lower in women than in men. Body fatness at young adulthood is particularly related to risk of renal cancer whereas attained body fatness at a later stage of adulthood is driving the risk for postmenopausal breast and endometrial cancer. Fat distribution is playing a strong role for risk of adenocarcinoma of the esophagus and to a lesser extent also for colon cancer. Prediagnostic body fatness plays also a role in cancer recurrence and survival.</AbstractText>
